  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery and RAP analysis show an \nunseasonably strong anticyclone over the central Appalachians with \nridging from lower Michigan into New England, while a negatively \ntilted trough is lifting rapidly northeast toward the Twin \nPorts/Thunder Bay, impinging upon this ridge. At the surface, a 1002 \nmb low is over the ND/MB border, with a strong pressure gradient \nbetween that feature and a 1030 mb high over the upper Ohio Valley. \nAs that low continues NE toward James Bay today, surface winds veer \nfrom southerly to southwesterly, while cold advection occurs in the \n925-850 mb layer from west to east. This will result in rather deep \nmixing into a strong wind field (35-45 kt at 925mb). The result will \nbe winds quickly increasing this morning, with gusts of 30-45 mph \ncommon from mid-morning into early afternoon before the pressure \ngradient gradually slackens. Strong consideration was given to a \nWind Advisory, but given the relatively short duration of 45 mph \nwind potential and the fact that leaves are off the trees, thereby \nreducing potential for limbs coming down, opted against it at this \ntime, but will continue to monitor. Another unseasonably warm day \nwith highs in the low 50s NW to low 60s SE, around 20F above climo \nand possibly exceeding records (see climate section below). Relative \nhumidity will drop to near 30 percent in the central UP which, \ncombined with the gusty winds, could lead to increased risk of \nwildfire spread should ignition occur.\n\nAnother unseasonably warm and less windy day is in store Friday as a \nridge of high pressure briefly builds in. However, southerly winds \nwill increase once again late in the day into Saturday as another \nlow takes nearly the same path as today's, albeit a bit stronger. \nThis will send highs into the upper 50s/low 60s, again near record \nvalues. The attendant midlevel wave is the lead portion of a full-\nlatitude trough shifting across the Intermountain West. As that \ntrough continues east, it will slowly drag a frontal boundary from \nwest to east across the UP Saturday into Saturday night, along which \nrain showers will occur, most widespread late Saturday into early \nSunday. Ensemble cluster analysis shows the amount of rain will \ndepend on whether a surface wave develops along the front, with a 30-\n40% chance of amounts exceeding an inch if this occurs. Snow would \nbegin to mix in later Sunday over the higher terrain of the west and \nnorth-central under this scenario as temps cool behind the front, \nand lake effect snow looks to occur Sunday night into Monday. \nHowever, the probability of any significant accumulation remains \nlow, with 1-3\" being the most likely outcome at this time over the \nN/NW wind belts by Monday evening. Temperatures finally return to \nseasonal normals early next week with highs ranging through the 30s \nand lows in the mid-teens to mid-20s.",
